<h2>Indian fresh fruit for export</h2>
<p>India's fresh fruit export business runs on a small number of products with genuinely strong global positions — grapes in the January–April window when Southern Hemisphere supply is ending, Bhagwa pomegranate for its deep red aril and long shelf life, and Cavendish banana as India's largest fruit export by value.</p>

<p>What all of them have in common is that arrival quality is decided before the container sails: how fast field heat was removed, whether the fruit was graded honestly, and whether the reefer is set correctly for that specific product.</p>

<h2>How we handle fresh fruit orders</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Count and grade agreed upfront.</strong> Fruit is graded by size/count to your specification, not to a vague "export quality" standard.</li>
  <li><strong>Prompt pre-cooling.</strong> Field heat is removed before loading — this is what determines whether you get full shelf life or half of it.</li>
  <li><strong>Product-specific reefer settings.</strong> 13–14°C for banana, 0–2°C for grapes, 5–7°C for pomegranate. Data loggers available on request.</li>
  <li><strong>Residue testing where it matters.</strong> For EU and UK shipments, pesticide residue is the most common rejection cause across Indian fresh produce — we arrange pre-shipment testing on request.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Seasonality is real — plan around it</h2>
<p>Unlike storable commodities, fresh fruit has hard seasonal windows. Grapes run January–April. Pomegranate is broadly September–March across staggered flowering cycles. Mango is a short, intense March–June. Banana is the outlier — available year-round from staggered regional harvests. Check our <a href="/resources/harvest-calendar/">harvest calendar</a> when planning a programme, and ask us for current-season availability before committing to delivery dates.</p>

